**Source of truth: catalog DB** (PostgreSQL or wherever products live).

**Hot tier: Redis prefix index** — one sorted set per `(locale, prefix)`:

```
KEY:    ac:{locale}:{prefix}            e.g. ac:en:ama
TYPE:   ZSET
MEMBER: product_id (or query string for query suggestions)
SCORE:  popularity score (clicks, conversions, recency-decayed)
MAX:    top 10 entries (additional entries trimmed)
```

For query `"ama"`, server does `ZREVRANGE ac:en:ama 0 9 WITHSCORES`. **One Redis call, one sorted set, top 10 returned.**

**Why one ZSET per prefix and not a trie?**

- Tries are conceptually elegant but a poor fit for Redis. A trie traversal is multiple node fetches; a ZSET lookup is one call.
- Redis sorted sets are O(log n) read/write, perfectly fast for our scale.
- The downside is storage duplication (each suggestion appears under each of its prefixes). Acceptable cost for the latency win.

**Auxiliary mapping** (Redis hash) for product metadata used to render suggestions:

```
KEY:  product:{product_id}
TYPE: HASH
FIELDS: name, image_url, category, current_price, in_stock
```

Letting the hot path fetch one ZSET + N hashes (~10) keeps the request to ~11 Redis calls, all pipelined.

**Cold tier: Elasticsearch** with edge n-gram tokenizer for typo-tolerant search:

```json
{
  "settings": {
    "analysis": {
      "tokenizer": {
        "edge_ngram_tokenizer": {
          "type": "edge_ngram",
          "min_gram": 2,
          "max_gram": 20,
          "token_chars": ["letter", "digit"]
        }
      },
      "analyzer": {
        "autocomplete_analyzer": {
          "tokenizer": "edge_ngram_tokenizer",
          "filter": ["lowercase", "asciifolding"]
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "name":     {"type": "text", "analyzer": "autocomplete_analyzer"},
      "category": {"type": "keyword"},
      "popularity": {"type": "float"}
    }
  }
}
```

**Why edge n-grams** — they index every prefix of every token at write time, so ES prefix queries are essentially term lookups. Fast at query time; index is larger.

Relationships:

```mermaid
erDiagram
    PRODUCTS ||--o{ POPULARITY_EVENTS : generates
    PRODUCTS ||--o{ PREFIX_ENTRIES : indexed_by
    PRODUCTS ||--o| ES_DOCUMENTS : indexed_in
    PRODUCTS {
        string product_id PK
        string name
        string category
        decimal price
        bool in_stock
        float popularity
    }
    POPULARITY_EVENTS {
        string event_id PK
        string product_id FK
        enum event_type
        timestamp ts
    }
    PREFIX_ENTRIES {
        string locale
        string prefix
        string product_id FK
        float score
    }
    ES_DOCUMENTS {
        string product_id PK
        text name_analyzed
        keyword category
        float popularity
    }
```

The order of operations matters for the latency budget:

```
def autocomplete(q, locale, user_id=None, limit=10):
    # 1. Hot path: Redis prefix lookup (target: 5ms)
    candidates = redis.zrevrange(
        f"ac:{locale}:{q.lower()[:20]}", 0, limit * 3, withscores=True
    )

    # 2. Fallback: ES if too few or query looks like typo (target: 20ms when used)
    if len(candidates) < 3 or looks_like_typo(q):
        es_hits = es.search(index=f"products_{locale}", body={
            "query": {"match": {"name": {"query": q, "fuzziness": "AUTO"}}},
            "size": limit * 2
        })
        candidates = merge_dedupe(candidates, es_hits)

    # 3. Personalization re-rank (target: 5ms when used)
    if user_id:
        candidates = personalize(candidates, user_id)

    # 4. Hydrate metadata (target: 10ms via pipelined Redis HGETs)
    return hydrate(candidates[:limit])
```

**`looks_like_typo` heuristic** — character not in dictionary, repeated characters, or very low ZSET hit count. Cheap to compute.

**Pipelined Redis** — hydration of 10 product hashes is one round-trip via `MULTI` or pipeline.

**Personalization is opt-in** — most calls are anonymous; personalized calls bypass the CDN.

Read path is the hottest code path; clean separation lets us A/B-test ranking strategies.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class AutocompleteController {
        +GET autocomplete(q, locale, userId, limit)
    }
    class AutocompleteService {
        +suggest(query, ctx) Suggestions
    }
    class PrefixIndex {
        <<interface>>
        +topByPrefix(locale, prefix, limit) List~Candidate~
    }
    class RedisPrefixIndex
    class FuzzyIndex {
        <<interface>>
        +typoSearch(locale, query, limit) List~Candidate~
    }
    class ElasticsearchFuzzyIndex
    class TypoDetector {
        +looksLikeTypo(q) boolean
    }
    class CandidateMerger {
        +merge(prefix, fuzzy) List~Candidate~
    }
    class PersonalizationRanker {
        +rerank(userId, candidates) List~Candidate~
    }
    class Hydrator {
        +hydrate(candidates) List~Suggestion~
    }
    class IndexBuilder {
        +onProductChange(event)
        +onPopularityUpdate(score)
    }
    class PrefixExpander {
        +expand(text, maxLen) List~String~
    }

    AutocompleteController --> AutocompleteService
    AutocompleteService --> PrefixIndex
    AutocompleteService --> FuzzyIndex
    AutocompleteService --> TypoDetector
    AutocompleteService --> CandidateMerger
    AutocompleteService --> PersonalizationRanker
    AutocompleteService --> Hydrator
    PrefixIndex <|.. RedisPrefixIndex
    FuzzyIndex <|.. ElasticsearchFuzzyIndex
    IndexBuilder --> PrefixExpander
    IndexBuilder --> PrefixIndex
    IndexBuilder --> FuzzyIndex
```

**Key code paths** (interesting bodies only):

```java
public class AutocompleteService {
    public Suggestions suggest(Query q, Context ctx) {
        List<Candidate> prefix = prefixIndex.topByPrefix(
            ctx.locale(), q.text(), q.limit() * 3);

        List<Candidate> fuzzy = (prefix.size() < 3 || typoDetector.looksLikeTypo(q.text()))
            ? fuzzyIndex.typoSearch(ctx.locale(), q.text(), q.limit() * 2)
            : List.of();

        List<Candidate> merged = merger.merge(prefix, fuzzy);

        List<Candidate> ranked = ctx.userId().isPresent()
            ? ranker.rerank(ctx.userId().get(), merged)
            : merged;

        return new Suggestions(hydrator.hydrate(ranked.subList(0, Math.min(q.limit(), ranked.size()))));
    }
}

public class IndexBuilder {
    public void onProductChange(ProductEvent ev) {
        Product p = ev.product();
        for (String token : tokenize(p.name())) {                     // "amazon basics" -> [amazon, basics]
            for (String prefix : expander.expand(token, 20)) {        // amazon -> [a, am, ama, amaz, ...]
                prefixIndex.upsert(p.locale(), prefix, p.id(), p.popularity());
            }
        }
        fuzzyIndex.upsert(p);
    }
}

public class RedisPrefixIndex implements PrefixIndex {
    @Override public List<Candidate> topByPrefix(String locale, String prefix, int limit) {
        return redis.opsForZSet().reverseRangeWithScores(
            "ac:" + locale + ":" + prefix.toLowerCase(), 0, limit - 1
        ).stream().map(t -> new Candidate(t.getValue(), t.getScore())).toList();
    }

    @Override public void upsert(String locale, String prefix, String id, double score) {
        String key = "ac:" + locale + ":" + prefix;
        redis.opsForZSet().add(key, id, score);
        redis.opsForZSet().removeRange(key, 0, -11);                  // keep top 10
    }
}
```

Why this shape:

- **`PrefixIndex` interface** — today Redis, tomorrow a custom in-memory trie service if Redis becomes a cost issue.
- **`FuzzyIndex` interface** — today Elasticsearch; could swap to Lucene-on-disk or a vector search backend for semantic matches.
- **`PersonalizationRanker` is its own collaborator** — absent for anonymous traffic, swappable.
- **`PrefixExpander` extracted** — the 1-character-at-a-time expansion logic is testable in isolation; lets us tune `min_gram`/`max_gram`.
